messages:
sam: Heads up — streaming responses from @redwood/inference-js SDK are hanging in our Vercel Edge deployment. Start of stream is fine, then mid-stream we see a 401 Unauthorized and the ReadableStream stops yielding. Node server runtime looks OK. Repro below.

maya: Which SDK version + runtime? can you paste minimal repro and request id? :wave:

sam: Sure — minimal edge handler (Next.js Edge):

```js
// Next.js Edge function
import { RedwoodClient } from '@redwood/inference-js';
export default async (req) => {
  const client = new RedwoodClient({ apiKey: process.env.RW_KEY });
  const res = await client.stream.create({ model: 'gpt-3.5-redwood', input: 'hello' });
  // expecting WHATWG ReadableStream in edge
  const reader = res.body.getReader();
  while (true) {
    const { done, value } = await reader.read();
    if (done) break;
    process.stdout.write(value);
  }
  return new Response('ok');
}
```

sam: Error from function logs: `TypeError: res.body.getReader is not a function` (edge). On some requests we also see a 401 mid-stream followed by `ERR_STREAM_PREMATURE_CLOSE` in the function logs. request_id: rwd_req_9f3b6

maya: What exact SDK version + Next/Vercel?

sam: @redwood/inference-js@1.3.0, Next 13.5, Vercel Edge runtime. Node version in local dev is 18.16.0.

jin: We saw a similar symptom in #eng-runtime a while back — root cause was mixing fetch/undici polyfills so `res.body` could be either a WHATWG ReadableStream or a Node Readable. Node Readable doesn't implement `getReader()`, so calling it risks a TypeError. The 401 mid-stream suggests the gateway returned auth error while the connection was open (token expired or rotation) — streaming connections can't be retried mid-flight, so the client just sees an abrupt close.

maya: Quick workaround: prefer async iterator and feature-detect instead of assuming `getReader()` exists. e.g.:

```js
// safe read that works across runtimes
if (res.body && typeof res.body.getReader === 'function') {
  const reader = res.body.getReader();
  // read via reader.read()
} else if (res.body && Symbol.asyncIterator in Object(res.body)) {
  for await (const chunk of res.body) {
    process.stdout.write(chunk);
  }
} else {
  // fallback for older Node stream.Readable
  res.body.on('data', (c) => process.stdout.write(c));
  await once(res.body, 'end');
}
```

sam: That fallback avoids the TypeError in staging — thanks. But we still see ~0.1% of requests where the server returns 401 partway through the stream, producing partial output. Billing flagged a potential double-charge if callers retry the entire request after a partial success. Does the SDK auto-retry streaming endpoints on 401?

support-bot: Attached gateway logs for rwd_req_9f3b6 and created ticket TKT-4821. Please tag eng-oncall.

eng-oncall: I'll pick this up. Initial checklist: 1) check gateway auth flow for token expiry/rotation windows, 2) confirm SDK runtime detection patch presence, 3) ensure SDK does NOT auto-retry streaming requests on 4xx/5xx by default.

maya: FYI we have an internal patch in the edge-runtime branch that does two things: (A) robust runtime detection so we don't call `getReader()` blindly, (B) disable automatic retries for streaming endpoints and bubble up a structured StreamError with a code field. Planning to release 1.3.2 in ~2 hours.

sam: That sounds good. Can you include a `streamError.code` that distinguishes auth vs network vs server? Right now it's opaque and app logic can't tell whether to surface partial output or re-initiate.

maya: Yep — adding `streamError.code` ∈ {"auth","network","server"} and `streamError.request_id`. Also adding migration note in the changelog showing the async-iterator fallback example.

sam: Patch deployed to staging and canary looks stable. Will run a canary on production traffic after you publish 1.3.2. :thumbsup:

jin: Follow-ups for infra: examine gateway token TTL and see if we can return a 429/short-lived response instead of 401 mid-stream, or finish stream with an explicit terminal control event so clients can detect auth termination more clearly.

sam: Thanks all — appreciate the quick turnaround. Will update ticket TKT-4821 with our canary results.

maya: Thanks for the repro and request ID, saved us time. ETA PR: ~90m.

sam: Great — ping me when released and I'll rerun canary.
